; AN ARRAY OF PAIRS, ON FOUR TARGETS.
; inputs: 0 1 2
; expect: 1079 1001 845
;
; products.md: a tuple is a function whose domain is a finite set, and
; tables.md §5.3 had already written the consequence — *"a statically-indexed
; heterogeneous `(array x y)` is a pair, and `(a 0)`/`(a 1)` are its
; projections; the tuple is not a separate feature."*
;
; So this program never says `stride`. It builds `(cap)` PAIRS, writes each with
; `(array a b)`, and reads a field with `((t i) j)`. The compiler generates
; `t[2i+j]` and proves the index it generated — emit/product.go.
;
; IT IS HERE BECAUSE THE PASS IS TARGET-INDEPENDENT AND THAT IS A CLAIM.
; Flattening happens before the checker, so the four backends never learn that
; products exist and none of them changed. A claim of that shape is exactly what
; the differential suite is for: if any target disagreed, the flattening would be
; emitting a different program somewhere.
;
; THE CHECKSUM IS POSITION-WEIGHTED AND THE FIELD DIFFERENCE IS ORDERED,
; deliberately. A weighted sum changes if any pair lands anywhere else, and
; `f1 - f0` makes a swapped projection — `(… 0)` read for `(… 1)` — change the
; answer rather than cancel. The expectations were computed by hand before the
; program was run.
;
; The `+ 14` is a bias that keeps every term positive, and it is here for a
; target rather than for the arithmetic: `win/fmt.print-int` prints a blank line
; for a negative number and is within its rights (refinements.md §6b names that
; exact gap). Without it windows built, ran, exited 0 and printed nothing.

(def cap (fn () 12))

; THERE IS NO CLAMP IN THIS PROGRAM, and that is the strongest thing it says.
; Every index is a loop variable the guard already bounds, and the STRIDE — the
; `2i` and `2i+1` the compiler generates from it — is proven from that same
; guard by the Farkas multiplier json-tree-bench added for exactly this shape.
; A hand-strided program writes the multiplication itself and then has to be
; believed about it; here the compiler writes it and discharges it.

; Field 0 is a counter, field 1 is a residue, so the two differ and a projection
; that reads the wrong one is visible in the answer.
(def mk (fn (s)
  (build (cap) (fn (t)
    (loop ((t t) (i 0))
      (>= i (cap))  t
      else (again (set t i (array (+ i s) (% (* 7 (+ i s)) 13))) (+ i 1)))))))

(def run (fn (s)
  (let (mk s) (fn (t)
    (loop ((acc 0) (r 0))
      (>= r (cap))  acc
      else (again (+ acc (* (+ r 1) (+ 14 (- ((t r) 1) ((t r) 0))))) (+ r 1)))))))
